WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.146
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.146 --> 00:00:05.912
Building Non-Person Identity.

3
00:00:05.912 --> 00:00:09.175
This is Chapter 9: AI agent identities.

4
00:00:09.175 --> 00:00:11.854
An agent can act at a speed no person can,

5
00:00:11.854 --> 00:00:16.711
so the questions the program asks of
every identity, who answers for it,

6
00:00:16.711 --> 00:00:20.144
what it holds, how it proves
itself and when it ends,

7
00:00:20.144 --> 00:00:25.000
are asked before it exists, written
down, and checked by code every week.

8
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.078
We'll cover what's generally
available and what's still preview,

9
00:00:29.078 --> 00:00:33.627
the program's decision as data, Learn's
samples and their gaps, the rules,

10
00:00:33.627 --> 00:00:38.725
the sponsor checks, the agent Conditional
Access plan, two labs and the dangers.

11
00:00:38.725 --> 00:00:44.240
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

12
00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:51.040
Why does this chapter come with
so many questions up front?

13
00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:56.277
Because an agent works at machine speed,
so whatever it can reach, it reaches fast.

14
00:00:56.277 --> 00:00:58.791
Microsoft's answer is Agent ID.

15
00:00:58.791 --> 00:01:02.724
A blueprint holds the credentials
and creates the agent identities,

16
00:01:02.724 --> 00:01:05.553
which hold the permissions
and show up in the logs.

17
00:01:05.553 --> 00:01:10.457
That structure has a sharp edge: the
blueprint gets tokens on behalf of every

18
00:01:10.457 --> 00:01:15.516
agent under it, so one compromised
blueprint credential is every agent at once.

19
00:01:15.516 --> 00:01:19.157
The platform became
generally available in April;

20
00:01:19.157 --> 00:01:22.630
the extra security
features need Agent 365.

21
00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:29.651
Before any code, you need to
know what's actually available,

22
00:01:29.651 --> 00:01:32.105
because the pieces are
at different stages.

23
00:01:32.105 --> 00:01:34.618
The core objects are generally available.

24
00:01:34.618 --> 00:01:38.445
Conditional Access for agents
and agent risk are preview,

25
00:01:38.445 --> 00:01:42.592
and the Graph properties for
agent policies exist only in beta.

26
00:01:42.592 --> 00:01:44.593
In this estate's G C C cloud,

27
00:01:44.593 --> 00:01:49.454
the documented subset is listed,
mostly for delegated access.

28
00:01:49.454 --> 00:01:52.743
In the higher government clouds,
nothing is published yet.

29
00:01:52.743 --> 00:01:57.939
So the program's decision is held as data:
in G C C the code reads agent identities,

30
00:01:57.939 --> 00:02:03.420
and elsewhere it falls back to applying
the same rules to service principals.

31
00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:13.057
Here are the program's rules, from Book 17,
each held as a finding with a stable ID.

32
00:02:13.057 --> 00:02:16.645
Creation is closed, so an
agent can't just appear.

33
00:02:16.645 --> 00:02:20.549
Every production agent has a
reviewed handshake before it exists,

34
00:02:20.549 --> 00:02:23.033
naming who answers for
it and what it may do.

35
00:02:23.033 --> 00:02:27.519
Credentials prefer a managed identity,
and never a production secret.

36
00:02:27.519 --> 00:02:31.399
Inheritance never hands every
future grant to every agent,

37
00:02:31.399 --> 00:02:34.408
and no agent holds a
standing privileged role.

38
00:02:34.408 --> 00:02:39.720
Sponsors are real employees, and a transfer
has ten business days to be confirmed.

39
00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:42.770
And drift is watched, with priorities.

40
00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:49.908
Microsoft gives you more samples
here than in most chapters.

41
00:02:49.908 --> 00:02:53.561
There's a walk-through that
creates a blueprint with a sponsor,

42
00:02:53.561 --> 00:02:57.358
an owner and credentials; samples
for inheritable permissions;

43
00:02:57.358 --> 00:02:59.865
three agent Conditional Access policies;

44
00:02:59.865 --> 00:03:03.733
and Graph requests to list
blueprints, agents and their sponsors.

45
00:03:03.733 --> 00:03:07.637
There's also a PowerShell module,
but the chapter calls Graph

46
00:03:07.637 --> 00:03:11.307
directly so every request sits
in its reviewed call list.

47
00:03:11.307 --> 00:03:17.017
Two sets of pages are pinned, eleven files
in all, and a harness sends every sample,

48
00:03:17.017 --> 00:03:22.650
unmodified, to a fake Graph so the
chapter's own checks can run on the result.

49
00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:29.688
The list requests are a good
model, but they stop short.

50
00:03:29.688 --> 00:03:33.608
They read one page, and a page
holds at most a hundred agents,

51
00:03:33.608 --> 00:03:35.650
so a bigger tenant is half-read.

52
00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:37.900
They hard-code the worldwide host.

53
00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:40.561
The agent sponsor
request returns IDs only,

54
00:03:40.561 --> 00:03:43.665
and names only a write
permission for what is a read,

55
00:03:43.665 --> 00:03:48.100
so the code expands sponsors on the
list under a read permission instead.

56
00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:52.069
And the blueprint sample makes
the same person sponsor and owner,

57
00:03:52.069 --> 00:03:56.112
meaning whoever decides an agent
should exist also holds its keys.

58
00:03:56.112 --> 00:03:57.800
The code flags that.

59
00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:03.662
The rest of the gaps are about safety.

60
00:04:03.662 --> 00:04:07.503
The blueprint sample adds a password
next to the managed identity,

61
00:04:07.503 --> 00:04:10.575
which Learn itself doesn't
recommend for production.

62
00:04:10.575 --> 00:04:13.380
The inheritance sample
starts with allow-all,

63
00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:17.513
so every future grant to the
blueprint quietly reaches every agent.

64
00:04:17.513 --> 00:04:23.162
The pages disagree on a path and a limit,
so the code follows the API reference.

65
00:04:23.162 --> 00:04:26.215
And the block-all-agents
policy has no exclusion,

66
00:04:26.215 --> 00:04:29.725
so if you enforced it, you'd
block the approved agents too.

67
00:04:29.725 --> 00:04:33.094
So the verdict: use the reads
and the credential model,

68
00:04:33.094 --> 00:04:36.740
and never enforce that
block without its exclusion.

69
00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:41.715
What does the code add?

70
00:04:41.715 --> 00:04:46.024
Its own module, a read-only entry
script and a separate write path,

71
00:04:46.024 --> 00:04:50.740
plus the decision table, the program's
rules and two reviewed policies.

72
00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:54.102
It builds on Chapters 1
and 2 and changes neither.

73
00:04:54.102 --> 00:04:56.824
The inputs are where accountability lives:

74
00:04:56.824 --> 00:05:00.831
the Chief AI Officer's use-case
inventory, the handshake records,

75
00:05:00.831 --> 00:05:04.913
HR's mover and leaver events with
each new sponsor's confirmation,

76
00:05:04.913 --> 00:05:08.164
and the change board's
approved credential changes.

77
00:05:08.164 --> 00:05:10.618
One setting has no Graph property at all,

78
00:05:10.618 --> 00:05:15.520
so a person reads it in the admin center
and records the reading with a date.

79
00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:22.708
There are twenty-three rules, so
let's take them in two halves.

80
00:05:22.708 --> 00:05:26.970
The first half covers how an agent
comes to exist and who answers for it.

81
00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:30.820
Creation must be closed, with
a recent reading to prove it.

82
00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:34.522
Every identity with a record
needs a complete handshake,

83
00:05:34.522 --> 00:05:37.357
made before the agent
was created, not after.

84
00:05:37.357 --> 00:05:41.960
Every agent needs a real sponsor, an
enabled employee, plus a group, and a

85
00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:47.357
technical owner who isn't the sponsor, and
the directory must agree with the record.

86
00:05:47.357 --> 00:05:50.410
Then the credential rules:
no production secrets,

87
00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:53.000
and a certificate only with a reason.

88
00:05:56.750 --> 00:06:00.737
The second half is about what an
agent holds and how it's watched.

89
00:06:00.737 --> 00:06:03.364
Inheritance and directory
roles come first:

90
00:06:03.364 --> 00:06:07.340
any directory role is a finding unless
it's recorded with an end date,

91
00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:10.038
because Microsoft's list
of roles an agent may

92
00:06:10.038 --> 00:06:13.162
hold includes some the
program counts as privileged.

93
00:06:13.162 --> 00:06:17.900
Then the sponsor clock: ten business
days, counted around federal holidays,

94
00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:21.492
and an unconfirmed transfer
means the agent is orphaned

95
00:06:21.492 --> 00:06:24.625
and goes to Chapter 2's
rule, where a person decides.

96
00:06:24.625 --> 00:06:27.482
Then the two policies,
the approval attribute,

97
00:06:27.482 --> 00:06:30.310
and the three top-priority drift checks.

98
00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:38.872
The only thing this chapter ever writes
is the two agent policies, report-only.

99
00:06:38.872 --> 00:06:41.895
The plan compares the reviewed
files with the tenant,

100
00:06:41.895 --> 00:06:45.124
and where the program's
decision says agent Conditional

101
00:06:45.124 --> 00:06:48.422
Access isn't published, it
simply marks them not available.

102
00:06:48.422 --> 00:06:52.975
The write path refuses unless the plan
is the approved one, recent, and for

103
00:06:52.975 --> 00:06:57.984
this tenant, and every write creates a
report-only block policy and nothing else.

104
00:06:57.984 --> 00:07:01.998
It runs as a person with the
Conditional Access administrator role.

105
00:07:01.998 --> 00:07:06.850
And it never turns a policy on; that
waits until the feature leaves preview.

106
00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:11.650
Who's involved?

107
00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:13.037
Developers build it.

108
00:07:13.037 --> 00:07:17.375
The identity governance officer keeps
the handshakes and confirms orphans.

109
00:07:17.375 --> 00:07:22.156
The Chief AI Officer's staff keep the
use-case inventory, security operations

110
00:07:22.156 --> 00:07:26.488
own the drift findings, and the
security officers review the evidence.

111
00:07:26.488 --> 00:07:29.850
The read task holds ten read
permissions and nothing

112
00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:32.600
that ends in read-write;
a test enforces that.

113
00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:36.444
A person running it also needs a
reader role for custom attributes,

114
00:07:36.444 --> 00:07:39.938
and the agent administrator role
unless they own the agents.

115
00:07:39.938 --> 00:07:45.360
The write task holds only the policy
permissions, in a person's signed-in session.

116
00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:53.873
Lab 1 runs on a laptop, with
Chapters 1 and 2's output in place.

117
00:07:53.873 --> 00:07:58.859
First, send Learn's samples to the fake,
and read the one test that matters most:

118
00:07:58.859 --> 00:08:03.134
the block-all-agents policy has
no exclusion for approved agents.

119
00:08:03.134 --> 00:08:08.531
Then list each task's permissions, and
read the decision table for each cloud:

120
00:08:08.531 --> 00:08:13.498
agent mode in the worldwide and G C C
clouds, fallback in the higher ones.

121
00:08:13.498 --> 00:08:15.327
Then run the check in G C C.

122
00:08:15.327 --> 00:08:18.436
Eight AI identities, twenty-two findings,

123
00:08:18.436 --> 00:08:23.730
four of them top priority, and two
orphan candidates for Chapter 2.

124
00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:32.467
Reading the findings, the fictional tenant
shows almost every way this can go wrong.

125
00:08:32.467 --> 00:08:37.011
One agent's handshake is incomplete and
was reviewed a month after the agent

126
00:08:37.011 --> 00:08:41.267
already existed, and it holds a mail
administrator role with no end date.

127
00:08:41.267 --> 00:08:44.954
Its blueprint has a secret,
inherits every Graph role,

128
00:08:44.954 --> 00:08:48.305
and had a secret added
outside any change window.

129
00:08:48.305 --> 00:08:50.883
Another agent's sponsor left and nobody

130
00:08:50.883 --> 00:08:53.617
confirmed a new one in
time, so it's orphaned.

131
00:08:53.617 --> 00:08:56.165
There's a shadow agent nobody declared,

132
00:08:56.165 --> 00:08:59.030
and a piloted system
with no identity at all.

133
00:08:59.030 --> 00:09:02.430
One agent passes everything,
which is the target.

134
00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:10.355
Next, run the same estate as
if it lived in G C C High.

135
00:09:10.355 --> 00:09:15.167
The code switches to fallback mode and
sends no agent identity read at all.

136
00:09:15.167 --> 00:09:20.125
The one ordinary service principal gets
the same credential and permission findings

137
00:09:20.125 --> 00:09:24.464
it got before, and three records that
name agent identities become findings,

138
00:09:24.464 --> 00:09:26.530
because that cloud doesn't have them.

139
00:09:26.530 --> 00:09:30.762
Back in G C C, the dry run
would send exactly one request:

140
00:09:30.762 --> 00:09:35.667
a report-only high-risk policy,
since the other already exists.

141
00:09:35.667 --> 00:09:38.621
Applying it to the fake
creates it report-only,

142
00:09:38.621 --> 00:09:41.400
and a hash nobody approved is refused.

143
00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:49.950
Lab 2 uses a licensed test tenant with
fictional people, never production.

144
00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:52.830
Read the blueprint-creation
setting yourself,

145
00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:57.187
since there's no Graph property for it,
set it to No, and record the reading.

146
00:09:57.187 --> 00:10:01.015
Then build one properly governed
agent from Learn's sample:

147
00:10:01.015 --> 00:10:04.842
a sponsor who isn't the owner,
a managed identity credential,

148
00:10:04.842 --> 00:10:08.513
a handshake, an inventory row,
and the approval attribute.

149
00:10:08.513 --> 00:10:11.026
Run the check with read permissions only,

150
00:10:11.026 --> 00:10:14.574
compare the policy shape with
one made in the admin center,

151
00:10:14.574 --> 00:10:18.713
apply the plan as a person, and
confirm both policies are report-only.

152
00:10:18.713 --> 00:10:20.750
Then remove it all by hand.

153
00:10:24.500 --> 00:10:27.337
Agents make every familiar danger faster.

154
00:10:27.337 --> 00:10:32.038
Open creation means whoever builds an
agent becomes its sponsor and owner.

155
00:10:32.038 --> 00:10:36.938
A secret on a blueprint isn't one
credential, it's every agent made from it.

156
00:10:36.938 --> 00:10:42.113
Allow-all inheritance turns one future
grant into a grant to every agent.

157
00:10:42.113 --> 00:10:46.138
A sponsor who leaves unnoticed
leaves an agent nobody answers for.

158
00:10:46.138 --> 00:10:50.688
And preview features bring their own risks:
enforcing a policy on a feature that

159
00:10:50.688 --> 00:10:55.376
can still change, or assuming the higher
government clouds have objects they don't.

160
00:10:55.376 --> 00:10:58.610
Each danger has a guard
that runs every week.

161
00:11:02.360 --> 00:11:03.710
Here's what done looks like.

162
00:11:03.710 --> 00:11:06.785
Creation is closed and
the reading is recent.

163
00:11:06.785 --> 00:11:11.059
Every production agent had its
handshake reviewed before it existed,

164
00:11:11.059 --> 00:11:14.522
with a real employee sponsor,
a group, and a separate owner.

165
00:11:14.522 --> 00:11:19.018
No production blueprint holds a
secret, nothing inherits every role,

166
00:11:19.018 --> 00:11:21.935
and no agent holds a
standing directory role.

167
00:11:21.935 --> 00:11:26.560
Every sponsor transfer was confirmed
on time, or handed to the orphan rule.

168
00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:31.985
Both policies are report-only and reviewed,
and no top-priority drift is open.

169
00:11:31.985 --> 00:11:34.605
Next, Chapter 10 brings it all together:

170
00:11:34.605 --> 00:11:37.635
drift and evidence for
the security officer.

171
00:11:37.635 --> 00:11:38.790
Thanks for watching.
